# ===========================================================================
# openclaw-file-chat — line ending policy
# ===========================================================================
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# Without it, how a file is stored depends on each contributor's local
# `core.autocrlf` setting. That is exactly how a repo ends up with half its
# files CRLF and half LF. This file pins the behaviour instead of trusting
# every machine's git config.
#
# `eol=lf` (not a bare `text=auto`) is deliberate:
#   1. The plugin's runtime target is a Linux container, so LF is what the
#      host actually loads. Forcing LF everywhere means what you edit on
#      Windows is byte-identical to what runs in the container.
#   2. It overrides a local `core.autocrlf=true`, so git will not silently
#      rewrite the working tree to CRLF on the next checkout.
#
# Side effect: with this file in place, `git add` no longer prints
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" for every file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Never normalize binaries ----------------------------------------------
# `text=auto` already detects binary content, but listing the types this
# project is likely to gain (a logo, a screenshot, a packaged tarball) makes
# the intent explicit and immune to detection edge cases.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.svg   text
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.tgz   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.wasm  binary

# --- Windows-only scripts must keep CRLF to stay runnable -------------------
# None in this repo today. Uncomment if you ever add one.
# *.bat  text eol=crlf
# *.cmd  text eol=crlf
